{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1795543747,"nonce":"F1qFhilE7nYzYp8OsC4SEuu59e4=","target":"d7f66c46dec4bab53954fa91ed1520a409c786f62f10148087a11d8bf754407a","message":"`jno.solve.ptc` could not reach the steady state of Mach 3 flow over a 15 degree ramp. The setup is steady compressible Euler in `jno.fvm` (HLL flux, minmod limiter, 749 cells) started from the freestream. Newton alone stalls at |R| 8e-4. `ptc` with D equal to the row absolute sums (a local CFL number) takes stable steps at CFL 1 while the shock forms. Then density goes negative (rho_min -0.7 at step 29) and the march never recovers, also with the CFL floored and rejected steps cut by 10. The explicit `erk` march of the same scheme reaches the steady state.\n\n**Cause.** The implicit step has no positivity guarantee: one Newton update across the forming shock can leave the admissible set, and a cut step is not enough to bring it back.\n\n**Fix.** Two commits in #345. Commit d4a0bb21 adds `ptc(positive=[...], keep=0.2)`: expressions of the unknowns that must stay positive, and each step is cut back so that they do (fraction to the boundary). Commit 0869406d steps each control volume as one (one damping factor per cell, shared by its components) and ramps the pseudo-time step (`ramp=1.2`). The wedge now converges in 80 steps. The test is `tests/test_solve_ptc.py::test_ptc_reaches_the_steady_supersonic_wedge`. Commit 43e5af60 made the result differentiable through `custom_root` (tested against a closed form in 40a6d4af). The code is in `jno/utils/solver/ptc.py`.","files":null}]}